    "texte": [
      "Ladies and gentlemen,",
      "We are gathered here today to examine an invisible yet omnipresent phenomenon: the tyranny of immediacy. Our era, characterized by relentless digitalization, appears to have elevated speed into a cardinal virtue. We are expected to move ever faster, reply instantaneously to emails, and consume information the very second it is produced. Yet, at what cost? Although this acceleration undoubtedly fosters a certain degree of productivity, it profoundly alienates the individual.",
      "Indeed, it is undeniable that extended periods of time—those dedicated to deep reflection and intellectual maturation—are now viewed as anomalies, or worse, as a drain on profitability. Consequently, we suffer from permanent cognitive overload. Chronic anxiety and professional burnout are becoming normalized at an alarming rate. Contemporary employees are demanded absolute responsiveness, thereby obliterating the clear boundary that once separated the professional sphere from the intimacy of home life.",
      "Even though some technophiles argue that modern tools liberate us from tedious tasks, lived experience proves otherwise. We have not gained free time; rather, we have simply condensed our hours. In other words, we perform multiple tasks simultaneously without ever truly savoring the present moment. It is therefore imperative that we rehabilitate the praise of slowness. Ultimately, slowing down does not mean turning our backs on progress; instead, it means reclaiming control over our own existence in the face of a system spiraling out of control. Thank you."
