"Dr. Pendelton: Thank you for having me. The answer lies in how modern marketing has evolved. Historically, advertisements highlighted a product's practical utility. Today, however, corporations sell identity and emotional relief. Whenever people experience stress or inadequacy, purchasing an item triggers a brief surge of dopamine. Unfortunately, that satisfaction evaporates quickly, trapping consumers in a perpetual cycle of acquisition.",
"Interviewer: Yet, many argue that high consumption drives economic growth and creates jobs. Isn't there a positive side to this dynamic?",
"Dr. Pendelton: On a macroeconomic level, consumer spending certainly fuels short-term growth. Nevertheless, when we examine the consequences on our daily lives, the toll is severe. Households are drowning in debt to finance lifestyles they cannot sustain. Furthermore, our living spaces are cluttered with unused goods, which ironically generates visual stress and mental fatigue. Not to mention the devastating environmental footprint caused by planned obsolescence—the deliberate manufacturing of products designed to break down prematurely.",
"Interviewer: So, how can an ordinary individual break free from this conditioning when every digital platform is tailored to tempt us?",
"Dr. Pendelton: Awareness is the first step. Before making any non-essential purchase, I advise people to impose a mandatory forty-eight-hour waiting period. More often than not, the impulse subsides, revealing that the desire was manufactured rather than genuine."
